inks with one query, incl. a small-business loan application and a CEO revenue review.\n\nRule: money and client details never go in a personal AI assistant.\n\nhttps://t.co/LYvXFmDgVQ", "url": "https://x.com/RickClarkeNGD/status/2116277382835286458"}, {"author_id": "1753029500302983168", "created_at": "2026-10-30T21:11:15.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2116276772928913830"], "id": "2116276772928913830", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 1, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "@AEON_Community @Meta @Muse PayPal are moving into the same territory, while Amazon has pushed back against third-party AI checkout. Read together, these moves point to a layer that is becoming increasingly important: the space between an", "url": "https://x.com/favrick12VAULTD/status/2116276772928913830"}, {"author_id": "1036360729283178502", "created_at": "2026-10-30T21:07:19.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2116275781315989594"], "id": "2116275781315989594", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 47, "like_count": 2, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "@soleio @spc @alexcornell @Meta @Muse oh, wow, dream design chat!", "url": "https://x.com/nonieengel/status/2116275781315989594"}, {"author_id": "1720727135466500096", "created_at": "2026-10-30T20:52:09.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2116271965103079805"], "id": "2116271965103079805", "note_tweet": {"text": "Alexandr Wang just showed what personal superintelligence actually looks like in real life: a finished errand, on a real bill.\n\nA user let Meta Muse compare insurance and walk away with the better deal. One real bill beats 100 benchmark scores, because benchmarks never have to pay the renewal.\n\nMost AI still stops at drafting the email. This crossed the line into doing the job.\n\nWhat is the one boring errand you would actually let AI finish end to end? Insurance, flights, refunds, pick one."}, "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 1, "impression_count": 20, "like_count": 1, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "Alexandr Wang just showed what personal superintelligence actually looks like in real life: a finished errand, on a real bill.\n\nA user let Meta Muse compare insurance and walk away with the better deal. One real bill beats 100 benchmark scores, because benchmarks never have to https://t.co/hQg8E0xqnO", "url": "https://x.com/BasicProtein26/status/2116271965103079805"}, {"author_id": "16841829", "created_at": "2026-10-30T20:48:53.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2116271143003070793"], "id": "2116271143003070793", "note_tweet": {"text": "It\u2019s not that hard.\n\nIf Google is losing some of the surface area where advertisers historically went to reach users and drive revenue to their businesses, that advertising demand has to go somewhere else.\n\nOne of the most obvious places is Instagram and Facebook.\n\nNow assume the amount of advertising inventory on Instagram and Facebook stays relatively flat.\n\nMore advertiser demand chasing roughly the same supply means advertisers have to spend more to reach those users.\n\nThat\u2019s basically what we just saw in Meta\u2019s numbers.\n\nThe second part of this is Meta Muse becoming a personal agent that arguably has some of the most relevant, real-time insight into a consumer\u2019s preferences.\n\nThink about it.\n\nYou\u2019re already spending time on Instagram and Facebook. You see something you like. Meta already knows a lot about what you engage with, who you follow, what content keeps your attention, and what products are likely relevant to you.\n\nMeta has been trying for a decade to close the gap between discovery and actually getting you to transact.\n\nMeta Muse can potentially do that.\n\nSo what does that mean for an advertiser?\n\nNow you can advertise against a real audience that is already highly engaged on the platform, with incredible data around their preferences, and increasingly give those potential customers a way to convert without ever really leaving the Meta ecosystem.\n\nThat makes the advertising surface more valuable, not less."}, "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 28, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "@jarahmacf It\u2019s not that hard.\n\nIf Google is losing some of the surface area where advertisers historically went to reach users and drive revenue to their businesses, that advertising demand has to go somewhere else.\n\nOne of the most obvious places is Instagram and Facebook.\n\nNow assume the", "url": "https://x.com/_SeanDavid/status/2116271143003070793"}, {"author_id": "81996273", "created_at": "2026-10-30T20:38:36.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2116268556421836974"], "id": "2116268556421836974", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 23, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "@CalumLewis @CDarnton0 Agree with all, just pointing out that Meta Muse specifically which they are spearheading the future with. Doing a subscription trim is one of easy first use cases. The point is maybe they don\u2019t want to cancel Netflix specifically but it could be collateral and low friction.", "url": "https://x.com/50cash50stock/status/2116268556421836974"}, {"attachments": {"media_keys": ["3_2116266026547683328"]}, "author_id": "1992942932438183936", "created_at": "2026-10-30T20:28:34.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2116266031736049872"], "id": "2116266031736049872", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 40, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "\u4eca\u65e5\u306f\u8abf\u6574\u5c40\u9762\u3060\u3068\u601d\u3063\u3066\u308b\n\n\u307e\u3060\u4e0b\u304c\u308b\u306e\u304b\u3069\u3046\u304b\u306f\u5206\u304b\u3089\u306a\u3044\u3051\u3069\u3001\u5927\u5c40\u7684\u306b\u898b\u308c\u3070\u751f\u6210AI\u306f\u7523\u696d\u